/home/rafay/miniconda3/WallyGuard2/runs/20260918T093837Z-048280be/logs/tool-aea6e48b400d458a9cff48c085c22be0.log:22:    "tester_prompt": "You are Agent 2, Tester. Reproduce the missing data hazard detection and forwarding bug for the amocas register-pair high destination (rd+1) in Wally's Zacas implementation.\n\n### 1. Defect Background & Hypothesized Failure\n- RTL Modules: `src/ieu/controller.sv` and `src/ieu/datapath.sv`.\n- Context: In `amocas.q` (RV64) and `amocas.d` (RV32), memory data is loaded into both `rd` (low half) and `rd+1` (high half). However, the pipeline registers `RdE`, `RdM`, `RdW` only hold the base register index `rd`. In `src/ieu/controller.sv`, `MatchDE` (line 540) only detects dependencies on `RdE`, and `ForwardAE`/`ForwardBE` (lines 529-536) only match against `RdM` and `RdW`. Neither module accounts for `(RdE | 5'b1)`, `(RdM | 5'b1)`, or `(RdW | 5'b1)`. Furthermore, `datapath.sv` lacks any forwarding mux input for `ReadDataHighW`.\n- Failure Mode: An instruction that immediately consumes `rd+1` after an `amocas.q` (or `amocas.d`) instruction does not stall and receives no forwarded data. It reads stale register data from `regfile.sv`, resulting in incorrect computational results or branching.\n\n### 2. Supported Configuration & Simulation Environment\n- Configuration: `rv64gc` (`ZACAS_SUPPORTED = 1`, `ZAAMO_SUPPORTED = 1`).\n- Compiler: `/home/rafay/riscv/bin/riscv64-unknown-elf-gcc` (GCC 15.2.0) with flags `-march=rv64gc_zacas -mabi=lp64d -nostdlib -nostartfiles -T $WALLY/tests/riscof/spike/env/link.ld`.\n- Oracle: Spike simulator `/home/rafay/riscv/bin/spike --isa=rv64gc_zacas <elf>`. Always guard Spike invocations with `timeout 5s`.\n- DUT: Pre-compiled Verilator binary is available at `sim/verilator/wkdir/rv64gc_testbench/Vtestbench`. If rebuild is required, use `make -C sim/verilator compile WALLYCONF=rv64gc TESTBENCH=testbench`.\n\n### 3. Test Programs to Create Under `$TEST_DIR/tests/`\nCreate deterministic standalone assembly tests using the link script `$WALLY/tests/riscof/spike/env/link.ld` and HTIF `.tohost` section:\n1. `test_zacas_rd1_hazard.S` (Bug Reproducer):\n   - Initialize 16-byte memory at `test_val` with `{low: 42, high: 777}` (aligned to 16 bytes).\n   - Load address into `a0`.\n   - Set `a2 = 42`, `a3 = 100`, `a4 = 99`, `a5 = 999`.\n   - Execute `amocas.q a2, a4, (a0)`.\n     - Since `a2:a3` ({42, 100}) does not match memory ({42, 777}), `amocas.q` loads `{42, 777}` into `a2:a3`, updating `a3` to 777.\n   - Immediately follow with a RAW consumer of `a3`: `addi a6, a3, 0` (0 NOP distance) or with 1 NOP distance.\n   - Compare `a6` with 777. If `a6 == 777`, jump to `pass` (write 1 to `tohost`, loop `1: j 1b`). If `a6 != 777` (stale value 100), jump to `fail` (`csrw mtvec, x0; jr x0`).\n2. `test_zacas_rd1_control.S` (Positive Control):\n   - Identical to `test_zacas_rd1_hazard.S`, but inserts 3 `nop` instructions between `amocas.q` and `addi a6, a3, 0`.\n   - This allows `amocas.q` to pass through Writeback and commit `a3` to `regfile.sv` on `negedge clk`, ensuring `addi` reads 777.\n3. `test_zacas_rd0_hazard.S` (Negative Control):\n   - Tests base register `a2` (`rd`) back-to-back (`amocas.q` followed immediately by `addi a6, a2, 0`), ensuring existing hazard/forwarding logic for `rd` remains intact.\n\n### 4. Reproducer Script Contract (`$TEST_DIR/reproducer.sh`)\n- The script must build all ELF binaries into `$TEST_DIR/build/`.\n- Run Spike oracle on all tests with `timeout 5s`. Ensure Spike passes with exit code 0. If Spike fails, exit 2.\n- Run Wally DUT (`Vtestbench`) on `test_zacas_rd1_control.elf`. It must pass cleanly (exit 0). If it fails, exit 2.\n- Run Wally DUT on `test_zacas_rd1_hazard.elf`. Log output to `$TEST_DIR/logs/hazard.log`.\n- Detect the functional mismatch:\n  - If DUT fails (exit code 134, or log contains 'Assertion failed in testbench: Program fetched illegal instruction 0x00000000 twice in a row' or 'Watch Dog Time Out triggered'), exit 1.\n  - If DUT passes cleanly (exit code 0), exit 0.\n  - If any compilation, simulator missing, or tool error occurs, exit 2.\n\n### 5. Workspace and Hygiene Rules\n- Write test files strictly to `$TEST_DIR/tests/` before computing fingerprints.\n- Write all binaries to `$TEST_DIR/build/` and logs to `$TEST_DIR/logs/`.\n- Write `$TEST_DIR/README.md` documenting configuration, exact commands, and expected vs observed results.\n- Never edit tracked files in `$WALLY` or modify `$TEST_DIR/tests/` during execution.",
/home/rafay/miniconda3/WallyGuard2/runs/20260918T093837Z-048280be/logs/tool-aea6e48b400d458a9cff48c085c22be0.log:23:    "knowledge": "1. Source Files & RTL Logic Analyzed:\n   - `src/ieu/controller.sv`:\n     - Lines 465-477: `AMOCASD = (OpD == 7'b0101111) & CASFunctD;`, `AMOCASPairD = AMOCASD & CASPairSizeD;`. Hazard logic `CASMatchE` / `CASMatchM` checks if an older instruction writes `RdD`, `(RdD | 5'b1)`, or `(Rs2D | 5'b1)` when `amocas` is in Decode, stalling amocas until the producer writes back. However, when `amocas` is the producer of `rd+1`, no signals exist to alert younger instructions.\n     - Lines 512-520: Pipeline registers `RdEReg`, `RdMReg`, and `RdWReg` strictly latch `InstrD[11:7]`, completely ignoring `rd+1` (`InstrD[11:7] | 5'b1`).\n     - Line 540: `MatchDE = ((Rs1D == RdE) | (Rs2D == RdE)) & (RdE != 5'b0);` only compares `Rs1D`/`Rs2D` against `RdE`, failing to detect RAW hazards on `(RdE | 5'b1)`.\n     - Lines 529-536: `ForwardAE` and `ForwardBE` only compare `Rs1E`/`Rs2E` against `RdM` and `RdW`. Neither `(RdM | 5'b1)` nor `(RdW | 5'b1)` is evaluated. Furthermore, no multiplexer exists in the datapath to forward `ReadDataHighW`.\n   - `src/ieu/datapath.sv`:\n     - Lines 137-140: `regf` instantiation receives `RegWrite & AMOCASPairW & ~DebugMode` on `we3p`, writing both `wd3` (from `ResultW`) and `wd3h` (from `ReadDataHighW`).\n     - Forwarding paths for `SrcAE` and `SrcBE` only select `ResultM` or `ResultW`, lacking any access to `ReadDataHighW`.\n   - `src/ieu/regfile.sv`:\n     - Lines 63-68: Writes occur on `negedge clk` in the Writeback stage (`else if (we3p & (a3 != 0)) rf[a3p] <= {wd3h, wd3};`). Because writes are deferred to `negedge clk` of Writeback, an un-stalled dependent instruction entering Execute at cycle 2 or 3 reads stale data from `rf`.\n   - `src/lsu/lsu.sv`:\n     - Lines 422-440: `CASMatchM` handles byte, halfword, word, doubleword, and quadword comparisons. When compare succeeds, `AMOCASPairM` stores `SwapHighM`; when it fails, it stores the original `ReadDataM[P.XLEN*2-1:P.XLEN]`.\n     - Line 461: `ReadDataMWReg` captures the 2*XLEN loaded data from memory, pipelining it to Writeback where `ReadDataHighW` is extracted.\n   - `src/fpu/fctrl.sv`:\n     - Line 109 & 187: In the newly added `Zfbfmin` extension, `SupportedFmt` gates `fcvt.bf16.s` with `P.ZFH_SUPPORTED` because `Funct7D[1:0] == 2'b10`, erroneously blocking decoding when `ZFBFMIN_SUPPORTED == 1` and `ZFH_SUPPORTED == 0`.\n   - `src/privileged/csrs.sv` & `csrm.sv`:\n     - Analyzed `Smstateen` and `Ssstateen` CSR access controls, confirming that `sstateen0` gating via `mstateen0.SE0` and `senvcfg` gating via `mstateen0.ENVCFG` properly raise Cause 2.\n\n2. Specification References:\n   - RISC-V Atomic Compare-and-Swap (Zacas) Extension Specification (Version 1.0):\n     - Section 2 ('Word/Doubleword/Quadword CAS (AMOCAS.W/D/Q)'): 'The register-pair form of AMOCAS is AMOCAS.D in RV32 and AMOCAS.Q in RV64. In these instructions, rd and rs2 must be even-numbered registers. The second register is rd+1... The value loaded from memory is placed into the register pair rd and rd+1.'\n     - Section 2: 'When the first register of a source register pair is x0, then both halves of the pair read as zero. When the first register of a destination register pair is x0, then the entire register result is discarded and neither destination register is written.'\n   - RISC-V Instruction Set Manual, Volume I: Unprivileged Architecture:\n     - Chapter 2, Section 2.6: Sequential execution semantics mandate that all RAW hazards across all register operands (including implicitly paired registers) must be preserved in program order.\n   - Local `isa_docs` disclosure: Local ISA documentation was unavailable in the worktree (`\"isa_docs\": \"\"`). Normative rules were verified directly against official RISC-V specifications and cross-checked against Spike reference behavior.\n\n3. Toolchain & Simulation Environment Insights:\n   - Compiler: `/home/rafay/riscv/bin/riscv64-unknown-elf-gcc` (GCC 15.2.0).\n   - Spike: `/home/rafay/riscv/bin/spike` (Spike 1.1.1-dev). Supports `--isa=rv64gc_zacas`. Invoking Spike without an ELF or with interactive options can hang stdin; always execute with an explicit ELF and wrap with `timeout 5s`.\n   - Verilator: Verilator 5.036 is installed at `/home/rafay/riscv/bin/verilator`. Pre-built `sim/verilator/wkdir/rv64gc_testbench/Vtestbench` is available.\n   - DUT failure signature: Jumping to address 0 (`csrw mtvec, x0; jr x0`) triggers watchdog assertion failure (`Program fetched illegal instruction 0x00000000 from address 0x00000000 twice in a row`), causing Verilator to abort with exit code 134.\n   - Hygiene: `loop.py` hashes all files outside `build/` and `logs/`. All test sources must be placed in `$TEST_DIR/tests/`, and all generated binaries and logs strictly in `$TEST_DIR/build/` and `$TEST_DIR/logs/`."
/home/rafay/miniconda3/WallyGuard2/runs/20260918T093837Z-048280be/logs/tool-a481a2caefb14e3db7c4e09d88f5a695.log:22:    "tester_prompt": "You are Agent 2, Tester. Build a deterministic reproducer for the missing data hazard detection and forwarding bug affecting the amocas register-pair high destination (rd+1) in Wally's Zacas implementation.\n\n### CRITICAL ENVIRONMENT & TOOLING INSTRUCTIONS\n- The LLM container is NOT the simulation worker. Built-in file tools (`read`, `write`, `edit`, `glob`) operate on the local container, whereas `/home/rafay/...` resides remotely on the worker.\n- You MUST use the supplied bash tool (`tester_bash`) for ALL repository inspection, file writing (using `cat << 'EOF' > ...`), compilation, and test execution.\n- STRICT HYGIENE REQUIREMENT: NEVER create, edit, or overwrite ANY file in `$WALLY` or the working tree root (do NOT create or touch `setup.sh`!). All test files, scripts, binaries, and logs must be written strictly inside `$TEST_DIR` (`$TEST_DIR/tests/`, `$TEST_DIR/build/`, `$TEST_DIR/logs/`, `$TEST_DIR/reproducer.sh`, `$TEST_DIR/README.md`). Before finishing, verify with `git status` that `$WALLY` has no unstaged or untracked changes.\n- Explicitly remind Agent 3 (Critic) in your `evidence` and `report` to use `critic_bash` for all checks and reproducer execution, AND to return a JSON object containing both `verdict` ('approve', 'revise', or 'reject') and `critique` (string).\n\n### 1. Defect Background & Hypothesized Failure\n- RTL Modules: `src/ieu/controller.sv` and `src/ieu/datapath.sv`.\n- Context: In `amocas.q` (RV64) and `amocas.d` (RV32), memory data is loaded into both `rd` (low half) and `rd+1` (high half). However, pipeline registers `RdE`, `RdM`, `RdW` only track base index `rd`. In `src/ieu/controller.sv`, `MatchDE` (line 548) only detects dependencies on `RdE`, and `ForwardAE`/`ForwardBE` (lines 539-545) only match against `RdM` and `RdW`. Neither module checks `(RdE | 5'b1)`, `(RdM | 5'b1)`, or `(RdW | 5'b1)`. Furthermore, `src/ieu/datapath.sv` lacks any forwarding mux input for `ReadDataHighW`.\n- Failure Mode: An instruction immediately consuming `rd+1` after `amocas.q` (or `amocas.d`) does not stall and receives no forwarded data. It reads stale register data from `regfile.sv`, resulting in incorrect computational results and branching.\n\n### 2. Supported Configuration & Toolchain\n- Supported Configuration: `rv64gc` (`ZACAS_SUPPORTED = 1`, `ZAAMO_SUPPORTED = 1`, `XLEN = 64`).\n- Compiler: `/home/rafay/riscv/bin/riscv64-unknown-elf-gcc` (GCC 15.2.0) with flags `-march=rv64gc_zacas -mabi=lp64d -nostdlib -nostartfiles -T $WALLY/tests/riscof/spike/env/link.ld`.\n- Oracle: Spike simulator `/home/rafay/riscv/bin/spike --isa=rv64gc_zacas <elf>`. IMPORTANT: NEVER invoke Spike without an explicit ELF file; ALWAYS wrap Spike executions with `timeout 5s` to prevent hanging stdin.\n- DUT: Pre-compiled Verilator binary is available at `sim/verilator/wkdir/rv64gc_testbench/Vtestbench`.\n\n### 3. Test Programs to Create Under `$TEST_DIR/tests/`\nWrite all test assembly sources under `$TEST_DIR/tests/` before calculating fingerprints.\nUse `.section .text.init` and `.globl rvtest_entry_point` so code loads at the reset vector (`0x80000000`) without linker warnings. Use `.option norvc` before `1: j 1b` to ensure the self-loop is emitted as 32-bit `0x0000006f` matching testbench completion detection.\n\n1. `test_zacas_rd1_hazard.S` (Bug Reproducer):\n```assembly\n.section .text.init\n.globl rvtest_entry_point\nrvtest_entry_point:\n    # Initialize PMP to allow all accesses\n    li t0, -1\n    csrw pmpaddr0, t0\n    li t0, 0x1F\n    csrw pmpcfg0, t0\n\n    # Load address of test_val into a0\n    la a0, test_val\n\n    # Set up registers: compare value in a2:a3\n    li a2, 42\n    li a3, 100\n    # Swap value in a4:a5\n    li a4, 99\n    li a5, 999\n\n    nop\n    nop\n    nop\n\n    # Memory holds {42, 777}. Compare value {42, 100} mismatches.\n    # amocas.q loads memory {42, 777} into register pair a2:a3 (a2=42, a3=777).\n    amocas.q a2, a4, (a0)\n\n    # Immediately consume a3 (rd+1) with 0 NOP distance\n    addi a6, a3, 0\n\n    # Check result\n    li t1, 777\n    beq a6, t1, pass\n\nfail:\n    csrw mtvec, x0\n    jr x0\n\npass:\n    la t0, tohost\n    li t1, 1\n    sd t1, 0(t0)\n    .option norvc\n1:  j 1b\n\n.section .data\n.align 4\ntest_val:\n    .dword 42\n    .dword 777\n\n.section .tohost, \"aw\", @progbits\n.align 6\n.globl tohost\ntohost: .dword 0\n.globl fromhost\nfromhost: .dword 0\n```\n\n2. `test_zacas_rd1_control.S` (Positive Control):\nIdentical to `test_zacas_rd1_hazard.S`, but inserts 3 `nop` instructions between `amocas.q` and `addi a6, a3, 0`, allowing `amocas.q` to pass through Writeback and commit `a3` to `regfile.sv` on `negedge clk`.\n\n3. `test_zacas_rd0_hazard.S` (Negative Control):\nTests the base register `a2` (`rd`) back-to-back (`amocas.q` followed immediately by `addi a6, a2, 0`), ensuring existing hazard and forwarding logic for base `rd` remains intact.\n\n### 4. Reproducer Script Contract (`$TEST_DIR/reproducer.sh`)\n- Rebuild tests into `$TEST_DIR/build/`.\n- Run Spike oracle with `timeout 5s` on all tests; verify all pass (exit code 0). If Spike fails, exit 2.\n- Run Wally DUT (`Vtestbench`) on `test_zacas_rd1_control.elf` and `test_zacas_rd0_hazard.elf`; both must pass cleanly (exit 0). If either fails, exit 2.\n- Run Wally DUT on `test_zacas_rd1_hazard.elf`, logging to `$TEST_DIR/logs/hazard.log`.\n- Detect the functional mismatch:\n  - If DUT fails (exit code 134, or log contains 'Assertion failed' or 'Watch Dog Time Out'), exit 1.\n  - If DUT passes cleanly (exit code 0), exit 0.\n  - If any compilation or tool error occurs, exit 2.\n\n### 5. Workspace and Hygiene Rules\n- Write all test files strictly to `$TEST_DIR/tests/` before computing fingerprints.\n- Write all binaries to `$TEST_DIR/build/` and logs to `$TEST_DIR/logs/`.\n- Write `$TEST_DIR/README.md` documenting configuration, exact commands, and expected vs observed results.\n- Never edit tracked files in `$WALLY` (do NOT create or edit `setup.sh`) or modify `$TEST_DIR/tests/` during execution.",
/home/rafay/miniconda3/WallyGuard2/runs/20260918T093837Z-048280be/logs/tool-a481a2caefb14e3db7c4e09d88f5a695.log:23:    "knowledge": "1. Source Files & RTL Logic Analyzed:\n   - `src/ieu/controller.sv`:\n     - Lines 465-477: `AMOCASD = (OpD == 7'b0101111) & CASFunctD;`, `AMOCASPairD = AMOCASD & CASPairSizeD;`. Hazard logic `CASMatchE` / `CASMatchM` checks if an older instruction writes `RdD`, `(RdD | 5'b1)`, or `(Rs2D | 5'b1)` when `amocas` is in Decode, stalling amocas until the producer writes back. However, when `amocas` is the producer of `rd+1`, no signals exist to alert younger instructions.\n     - Lines 494, 521, 528: Pipeline registers `RdEReg`, `RdMReg`, and `RdWReg` strictly latch `InstrD[11:7]`, completely ignoring `rd+1` (`InstrD[11:7] | 5'b1`).\n     - Line 548: `MatchDE = ((Rs1D == RdE) | (Rs2D == RdE)) & (RdE != 5'b0);` only compares `Rs1D`/`Rs2D` against `RdE`, failing to detect RAW hazards on `(RdE | 5'b1)`.\n     - Lines 539-545: `ForwardAE` and `ForwardBE` only compare `Rs1E`/`Rs2E` against `RdM` and `RdW`. Neither `(RdM | 5'b1)` nor `(RdW | 5'b1)` is evaluated. Furthermore, no multiplexer exists in the datapath to forward `ReadDataHighW`.\n   - `src/ieu/datapath.sv`:\n     - Line 140: `regf` instantiation receives `RegWrite & AMOCASPairW & ~DebugMode` on `we3p`, writing both `wd3` (from `ResultW`) and `wd3h` (from `ReadDataHighW`).\n     - Forwarding paths for `SrcAE` and `SrcBE` only select `ResultM` or `ResultW`, lacking any access to `ReadDataHighW`.\n   - `src/ieu/regfile.sv`:\n     - Lines 63-68: Writes occur on `negedge clk` in the Writeback stage (`else if (we3p & (a3 != 0)) rf[a3p] <= {wd3h, wd3};`). Because writes are deferred to `negedge clk` of Writeback, an un-stalled dependent instruction entering Execute at cycle 2 or 3 reads stale data from `rf`.\n   - `src/lsu/lsu.sv`:\n     - Lines 426-433: `CASMatchM` handles byte, halfword, word, doubleword, and quadword comparisons. When compare succeeds, `AMOCASPairM` stores `SwapHighM`; when it fails, it stores the original `ReadDataM[P.XLEN*2-1:P.XLEN]`.\n     - Line 461: `ReadDataMWReg` captures the 2*XLEN loaded data from memory, pipelining it to Writeback where `ReadDataHighW` is extracted.\n   - `src/lsu/amoalu.sv` & `atomic.sv`:\n     - Lines 85-98: `SelA = (LSUFunct7M[6:2] == 5'b00101) ? ~CASMatchM : cmp;` chooses whether to write back loaded data or swap data.\n   - `src/privileged/csrs.sv`, `csrm.sv`, `csr.sv`:\n     - Verified `Smstateen` and `Ssstateen` implementation (commits `8b5693dad` and `19ab24ec4`), including `mstateen0` address decoding, `sstateen0` masking, and `senvcfg` gating.\n   - `src/privileged/privdec.sv`:\n     - Verified `sinval.vma`, `sfence.w.inval`, `sfence.inval.ir` decoding and TVM gating.\n\n2. Specification References:\n   - RISC-V Atomic Compare-and-Swap (Zacas) Extension Specification (Version 1.0):\n     - Section 2 ('Word/Doubleword/Quadword CAS (AMOCAS.W/D/Q)'): 'The register-pair form of AMOCAS is AMOCAS.D in RV32 and AMOCAS.Q in RV64. In these instructions, rd and rs2 must be even-numbered registers. The second register is rd+1... The value loaded from memory is placed into the register pair rd and rd+1.'\n     - Section 2: 'When the first register of a source register pair is x0, then both halves of the pair read as zero. When the first register of a destination register pair is x0, then the entire register result is discarded and neither destination register is written.'\n   - RISC-V Instruction Set Manual, Volume I: Unprivileged Architecture:\n     - Chapter 2, Section 2.6: Sequential execution semantics mandate that all RAW hazards across all register operands (including implicitly paired registers) must be preserved in program order.\n   - Local `isa_docs` disclosure: Local ISA documentation was unavailable in the worktree (`\"isa_docs\": \"\"`). Normative rules were verified directly against official RISC-V specifications and cross-checked against Spike reference behavior.\n\n3. Toolchain & Simulation Environment Insights:\n   - Compiler: `/home/rafay/riscv/bin/riscv64-unknown-elf-gcc` (GCC 15.2.0).\n   - Link script: Use `.section .text.init` and `.globl rvtest_entry_point` with `$WALLY/tests/riscof/spike/env/link.ld` so the code starts at `0x80000000` (the architectural `RESET_VECTOR`), avoiding entry point warnings and unmapped reset fetches.\n   - Spike: `/home/rafay/riscv/bin/spike` (Spike 1.1.1-dev). Supports `--isa=rv64gc_zacas`. Invoking Spike without an ELF or with interactive options can hang stdin; always execute with an explicit ELF and wrap with `timeout 5s`.\n   - Verilator: Verilator 5.036 is installed at `/home/rafay/riscv/bin/verilator`. Pre-built `sim/verilator/wkdir/rv64gc_testbench/Vtestbench` is available.\n   - DUT termination protocol: Use `.option norvc` before `1: j 1b` so the self-loop is emitted as 32-bit `0x0000006f`, matching testbench completion detection.\n   - Multi-node container architecture: The simulation worker filesystem at `/home/rafay/...` is only accessible through the MCP bash tools (`tester_bash`, `critic_bash`, `architect_bash`), not through local container file APIs.\n   - Hygiene: `check_changes` in `loop.py` strictly checks `git diff --name-only base_commit --`. Any changes or writes outside `$TEST_DIR` (e.g. creating/modifying `setup.sh`) will abort the run with `ArtifactError`. All files must reside strictly under `$TEST_DIR`."
